WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
Welcome to Session seven.

2
00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:05.675
This is the session where the course changes gear.

3
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:17.100
Everything up to now has been about comparing sequences and structures, and about scores you compute from data you already trust: alignment scores, profile scores, structure superpositions.

4
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:23.375
From today, the computer stops being told what to compute, and starts working it out from examples.

5
00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:31.075
This is the first machine learning in the course, and everything in Sessions eight to twelve is built directly on what we do this morning.

6
00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:36.000
The title has four things in it, and they are really one thing seen four ways.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.950
A linear model, which is a weighted sum.

8
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:45.125
Gradient descent, which is how such a model learns its weights from examples instead of being given them.

9
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:50.800
The XOR problem, which is the precise point where one weighted sum stops being enough.

10
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:59.650
And secondary-structure prediction, which is the biological problem we use to see all of it in action, and which is also your capstone project.

11
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.900
A word about what I expect of you after this.

12
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:12.075
You should be able to say what a weighted sum is, what gradient descent does to it, why one of them cannot solve XOR and what a hidden layer changes.

13
00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:16.350
You do not need to be able to write a PyTorch training loop from memory.

14
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:20.825
That comes in Session eight, and from then on a library does it for you.

15
00:01:20.825 --> 00:01:22.450
Three blocks.

16
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.800
First, the weighted sum and how it is learned.

17
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.900
Second, what one weighted sum can and cannot do, which is the XOR story.

18
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:37.850
Third, the biology, and a lesson about representation that I think is the most useful thing in the whole session.

19
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:44.000
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session seven page of the course book.

20
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.225
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

21
00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:56.250
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

22
00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.600
Before we start, look at this slide, because it is the map for the whole morning.

23
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.950
There are two pictures on it.

24
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:11.775
The top one is a single weighted sum: inputs come in, each is multiplied by a weight, they are added up, and something comes out.

25
00:02:11.775 --> 00:02:19.625
The bottom one has a layer in the middle: the inputs go to several weighted sums, and their outputs go into another weighted sum.

26
00:02:19.625 --> 00:02:21.300
That is it.

27
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.700
Those are the two pictures.

28
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.975
Now here is the point.

29
00:02:25.975 --> 00:02:31.450
Today you will hear a lot of names, and they can feel like a lot of different methods to learn.

30
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.525
A position-specific scoring matrix.

31
00:02:34.525 --> 00:02:36.200
A perceptron.

32
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.200
Linear regression.

33
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.450
Logistic regression.

34
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.000
The sigmoid.

35
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.300
The hyperbolic tangent.

36
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.100
A hidden layer.

37
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.525
A multilayer perceptron.

38
00:02:48.525 --> 00:02:50.275
PHD.

39
00:02:50.275 --> 00:02:55.800
Every single one of them is one of these two pictures, or a small variation on one.

40
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.375
So as each name goes by, I want you to ask yourself one question: is this picture one, or picture two?

41
00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:05.950
If you can answer that, the session has done its job.

42
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:16.200
And at the very end we will come back to this slide, and the question I will ask you is: what exactly changed between the two pictures, and why does that change allow XOR?

43
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.775
Do not worry if XOR means nothing to you yet.

44
00:03:19.775 --> 00:03:21.800
That is Block two.

45
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.875
Block one.

46
00:03:23.875 --> 00:03:26.875
A weighted sum, learned from examples.

47
00:03:26.875 --> 00:03:34.975
On the page, this block is the sections "A profile score and a perceptron: the same weighted sum" and "Learning the weights from examples".

48
00:03:34.975 --> 00:03:37.700
In this block we do three things.

49
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:44.450
We show that you already know what a neural network computes, because you met it in Session five under another name.

50
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:51.050
Then we replace counting with learning: gradient descent, step by step, with the arithmetic written out.

51
00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:56.250
And then we look at what sits after the weighted sum, which is the activation function.

52
00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:02.675
Let us start with something you already know, because the core of a neural network is not new to you.

53
00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:09.525
In Session five you built a position-specific scoring matrix, a PSSM, to search a database.

54
00:04:09.525 --> 00:04:18.200
You scored a candidate stretch of sequence by taking each position, looking up the score for the residue that was there, and adding all those scores up.

55
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.825
One number out, and if it was big enough, you had a hit.

56
00:04:21.825 --> 00:04:29.875
Write that as a formula and it looks like this: z equals w one x one, plus w two x two, and so on, plus b.

57
00:04:29.875 --> 00:04:36.825
Each x is something about the input at one position, each w is the weight you attach to it, and you add them up.

58
00:04:36.825 --> 00:04:43.150
In Session one you met this same operation under its mathematical name: it is a dot product.

59
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:48.850
Now here is the perceptron, which is the thing people call the simplest neural network.

60
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:55.200
It computes exactly that sum, and then does one more thing to it, which we will come to in a moment.

61
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.700
So the core of a neural network is a weighted sum.

62
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.425
You have been computing weighted sums since Session five.

63
00:05:02.425 --> 00:05:05.200
What is new is not the arithmetic.

64
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.375
One piece of notation before we move on.

65
00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:15.300
That b at the end is called the bias, and it is just a number added on at the end, with no input attached to it.

66
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.425
It looks like an afterthought and it is not.

67
00:05:18.425 --> 00:05:21.275
Its job is to move the threshold.

68
00:05:21.275 --> 00:05:27.850
Without it, the model is forced to make its decision around zero; with it, the decision can sit anywhere.

69
00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:32.400
We will see exactly what it does geometrically two slides from now.

70
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.450
A short historical slide, because the name perceptron comes with some baggage that is worth clearing up.

71
00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:44.625
The diagram on this slide is Frank Rosenblatt's own, from nineteen fifty-eight.

72
00:05:44.625 --> 00:05:54.150
At the top, a sketch of a brain circuit: inputs arriving at a cell through connections of different strengths, and the cell firing if enough of them arrive at once.

73
00:05:54.150 --> 00:06:02.425
At the bottom, the machine built to mirror it: the weights are the connection strengths, and the unit fires when the weighted sum crosses a threshold.

74
00:06:02.425 --> 00:06:13.875
He first simulated it on an IBM seven-oh-four in nineteen fifty-eight, and the Mark One Perceptron, which was actual hardware with motors adjusting the weights, followed in nineteen sixty.

75
00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:18.600
Now the caveat, and I want to be clear about it because it comes up every year.

76
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.500
The biological analogy is loose.

77
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:30.350
Real neurons are vastly more complicated than a weighted sum and a threshold, and nobody today claims a neural network is a model of the brain.

78
00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:38.275
The analogy was historically important, and it explains the vocabulary we are stuck with: neurons, firing, activation.

79
00:06:38.275 --> 00:06:41.450
But this course uses only the mathematics.

80
00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:45.575
When I say a unit fires, I mean a number crossed a threshold.

81
00:06:45.575 --> 00:06:47.325
Nothing more.

82
00:06:47.925 --> 00:06:53.400
Now the geometry, because this is the picture that makes everything in Block two obvious.

83
00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:59.875
Suppose you have two inputs, x one and x two, so you can draw each example as a point on a plane.

84
00:06:59.875 --> 00:07:09.550
The model computes z equals w one x one plus w two x two plus b, and decides one class if z is positive and the other if it is not.

85
00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.775
Where is the boundary between the two decisions?

86
00:07:12.775 --> 00:07:15.425
It is where z equals zero.

87
00:07:15.425 --> 00:07:19.325
And z equals zero is the equation of a straight line.

88
00:07:19.325 --> 00:07:22.000
You can see that by rearranging.

89
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:34.475
Solve w one x one plus w two x two plus b equals zero for x two, and you get x two equals minus w one over w two, times x one, minus b over w two.

90
00:07:34.475 --> 00:07:39.125
That is y equals m x plus c, the straight line you drew at school.

91
00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:45.200
The slope is set by the ratio of the weights, and the intercept by the bias divided by a weight.

92
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.600
So now you can read the parameters off the picture.

93
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.700
The weights set the slope, that is, the direction the line points.

94
00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.775
The bias slides the line sideways without changing its slope.

95
00:07:56.775 --> 00:08:00.800
That is what I meant when I said the bias moves the threshold.

96
00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.100
And in more dimensions the same thing happens with a different name.

97
00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.425
With three inputs the boundary is a flat plane.

98
00:08:08.425 --> 00:08:14.400
With twenty inputs it is a flat twenty-dimensional thing you cannot picture, called a hyperplane.

99
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.675
The important word in all of those is flat.

100
00:08:17.675 --> 00:08:21.075
A weighted sum always gives you a flat boundary.

101
00:08:21.075 --> 00:08:27.625
Remember that for Block two, because the whole of Block two is about a problem where a flat boundary is not enough.

102
00:08:28.225 --> 00:08:30.925
So we have a model with weights in it.

103
00:08:30.925 --> 00:08:33.200
Where do the weights come from?

104
00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.925
There are exactly two answers in this course, and this slide is the hinge of the whole semester.

105
00:08:38.925 --> 00:08:42.700
The first answer is the one you already know: count them.

106
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.450
That is what Session five did.

107
00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:55.625
You take a multiple alignment of sequences you trust, count how often each residue appears at each position, turn those counts into log-odds scores, and there are your weights.

108
00:08:55.625 --> 00:09:01.125
The pseudocounts you added were still counting, just with a correction for small samples.

109
00:09:01.125 --> 00:09:06.225
Counting works, and it is fast, and you can explain every number in the matrix.

110
00:09:06.225 --> 00:09:12.225
But look at what it needs: a trusted alignment of examples that are already known to be the same thing.

111
00:09:12.225 --> 00:09:15.450
For many questions you simply do not have that.

112
00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:21.125
The second answer, which everything from here to the end of the course uses, is to learn them.

113
00:09:21.125 --> 00:09:24.675
Start with arbitrary weights, usually random ones.

114
00:09:24.675 --> 00:09:29.475
Take labelled examples, that is, inputs where you know the right answer.

115
00:09:29.475 --> 00:09:32.500
Measure how wrong the model currently is.

116
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.975
Then nudge every weight a small step in the direction that makes it less wrong.

117
00:09:36.975 --> 00:09:39.225
Repeat, many times.

118
00:09:39.225 --> 00:09:44.175
That procedure is called gradient descent, and it is the next four slides.

119
00:09:44.175 --> 00:09:47.550
Notice what learning needs and what it does not.

120
00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.300
It does not need an alignment, or any prior idea of what the weights should be.

121
00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.575
It does need labelled examples, and a way of measuring wrongness.

122
00:09:56.575 --> 00:10:04.775
That trade, from needing curated knowledge to needing labelled data, is most of what changed in biology in the last fifteen years.

123
00:10:04.775 --> 00:10:08.450
One practical note, which the lab will make you feel.

124
00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:15.975
In Session seven's lab you do this with raw tensors and automatic differentiation, and you write the update yourself.

125
00:10:15.975 --> 00:10:19.925
From Session eight you will use a library that hides all of it.

126
00:10:19.925 --> 00:10:23.925
The point today is to see the machinery once, with the lid off.

127
00:10:24.525 --> 00:10:29.475
To nudge the weights in the right direction, you first have to measure how wrong you are.

128
00:10:29.475 --> 00:10:38.225
That measurement is the loss function, and it is a single number: small when the model's predictions are close to the labels, large when they are far away.

129
00:10:38.225 --> 00:10:43.225
The simplest one, and the one we use for the worked example, is the squared error.

130
00:10:43.225 --> 00:10:49.100
For one example, L of w equals the quantity w x minus y, all squared.

131
00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:54.400
In words: take the model's prediction, subtract the true value, and square the difference.

132
00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.175
Squaring does two useful things.

133
00:10:57.175 --> 00:11:03.200
It makes the sign irrelevant, since being too high by two is as bad as being too low by two.

134
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.925
And it punishes large errors much more than small ones.

135
00:11:06.925 --> 00:11:10.300
Now the requirement, and there is really only one.

136
00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.925
The loss has to be differentiable.

137
00:11:12.925 --> 00:11:22.800
That means it must have a well-defined slope everywhere, because the whole of gradient descent is walking downhill, and you cannot walk downhill on something with no slope.

138
00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:29.375
This single requirement has a consequence that will seem strange now and will make sense at the end of this block.

139
00:11:29.375 --> 00:11:35.325
The perceptron's original activation, the step function, is flat on both sides of zero.

140
00:11:35.325 --> 00:11:37.725
Flat means slope zero.

141
00:11:37.725 --> 00:11:45.400
Slope zero means gradient descent gets no signal at all: it cannot tell whether a small change to a weight helped or not.

142
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:51.150
So the original perceptron, with its step function, cannot be trained by gradient descent.

143
00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:58.850
That is why the sigmoid exists, and why every activation function in the rest of this course is a smoothed version of a step.

144
00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:00.850
Hold that thought.

145
00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.925
Now let us actually do it, on the smallest possible example, so that nothing is hidden.

146
00:12:06.925 --> 00:12:09.800
One input, one weight, no bias.

147
00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.700
The model is z equals w x.

148
00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:18.150
One training example: x equals two, and the correct answer y equals six.

149
00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:23.575
So the right weight is obviously three, and the question is whether the procedure finds it.

150
00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:27.825
We start with w equals zero, which is wrong on purpose.

151
00:12:27.825 --> 00:12:33.625
The loss is the squared error, L of w equals w x minus y, all squared.

152
00:12:33.625 --> 00:12:44.100
We need its slope with respect to w, and Session one's chain rule gives it: d L by d w equals two, times the quantity w x minus y, times x.

153
00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:49.100
Let me read that formula in words, because it is more intelligible than it looks.

154
00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:56.225
The middle bracket, w x minus y, is simply the error: how far the prediction is from the truth, with a sign.

155
00:12:56.225 --> 00:13:01.025
So the slope is proportional to the error, and proportional to the input.

156
00:13:01.025 --> 00:13:03.225
Big error, big step.

157
00:13:03.225 --> 00:13:05.625
Small error, small step.

158
00:13:05.625 --> 00:13:13.675
And then the update rule, which is the whole algorithm: the new w is the old w, minus a small number eta times the slope.

159
00:13:13.675 --> 00:13:16.875
That small number is called the learning rate.

160
00:13:16.875 --> 00:13:20.650
Watch the minus sign, because it catches everyone once.

161
00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:27.125
If the slope is negative, it means the loss goes down as w goes up, so we should increase w.

162
00:13:27.125 --> 00:13:30.475
Subtracting a negative number increases it.

163
00:13:30.475 --> 00:13:35.850
The minus sign is what makes "downhill on the loss" come out right in both directions.

164
00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:45.525
Starting at w equals zero, the sequence goes nought, then one point two, then one point nine two, then two point three five, and it keeps heading for three.

165
00:13:45.525 --> 00:13:49.225
And notice the thing I find most elegant about this.

166
00:13:49.225 --> 00:13:54.125
The steps get smaller as you approach the answer, and nobody arranged that.

167
00:13:54.125 --> 00:13:59.700
They get smaller because the gradient itself contains the error, and the error is shrinking.

168
00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.900
You never have to reduce the learning rate by hand to land softly.

169
00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:12.700
Let me put the actual numbers on the screen, because this is the kind of thing that is obvious once you have seen it once and mysterious until then.

170
00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:16.350
The learning rate is nought point zero five throughout.

171
00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:18.050
Step zero.

172
00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:23.400
w is zero, so the prediction w x is zero, and the target is six.

173
00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:28.925
The gradient is two, times nought minus six, times two, which is minus twenty-four.

174
00:14:28.925 --> 00:14:36.825
The update is w equals nought minus nought point zero five times minus twenty-four, which is plus one point two zero.

175
00:14:36.825 --> 00:14:40.300
So w moves from zero to one point two.

176
00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:41.775
Step one.

177
00:14:41.775 --> 00:14:47.050
w is one point two, so the prediction is two point four, still short of six.

178
00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:53.300
The gradient is two, times two point four minus six, times two, which is minus fourteen point four.

179
00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:57.100
Smaller in size than before, because the error is smaller.

180
00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:00.025
The update gives one point nine two.

181
00:15:00.025 --> 00:15:01.325
Step two.

182
00:15:01.325 --> 00:15:10.875
w is one point nine two, the prediction is three point eight four, the gradient is minus eight point six four, and w becomes two point three five.

183
00:15:10.875 --> 00:15:16.025
Look down that middle column: twenty-four, fourteen point four, eight point six four.

184
00:15:16.025 --> 00:15:22.150
The gradient is shrinking by itself, so the steps shrink by themselves, and eta never changed.

185
00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.650
That is the whole algorithm.

186
00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:31.325
Everything in the rest of this course that trains anything is this, done many times over, to many weights at once.

187
00:15:31.925 --> 00:15:42.125
The learning rate is the one number you chose by hand in that example, and it is worth a slide, because it is the setting that most often decides whether training works at all.

188
00:15:42.125 --> 00:15:48.725
The picture shows the same loss curve and the same starting point three times, with three different learning rates.

189
00:15:48.725 --> 00:15:50.975
Too small, on the left.

190
00:15:50.975 --> 00:15:55.900
Every step is in the right direction, so it does get there, but it takes forever.

191
00:15:55.900 --> 00:16:03.250
With eta equal to nought point zero one on our example, each step closes only eight per cent of the remaining distance.

192
00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:05.025
Good, in the middle.

193
00:16:05.025 --> 00:16:07.450
Nought point zero five.

194
00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:10.125
A handful of steps and you are there.

195
00:16:10.125 --> 00:16:14.000
Too large, on the right, and this is the interesting failure.

196
00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:19.900
Each step overshoots the minimum and lands further away on the other side than it started.

197
00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:22.725
The next step overshoots further still.

198
00:16:22.725 --> 00:16:26.500
The weight does not converge slowly; it flies apart.

199
00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:36.475
With eta equal to nought point two six, the distance to the answer is multiplied by minus one point zero eight every step: the sign flips, and the size grows.

200
00:16:36.475 --> 00:16:40.950
For this particular loss you can work out exactly where the boundary is.

201
00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:46.375
Each step multiplies the distance to w equals three by one minus eight eta.

202
00:16:46.375 --> 00:16:54.725
For that to shrink the distance, you need the size of one minus eight eta to be less than one, which means eta below nought point two five.

203
00:16:54.725 --> 00:17:01.650
So nought point two five is the largest learning rate that still converges, and nought point two six does not.

204
00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.950
That is a question worth sitting with for a minute.

205
00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:12.175
In real problems you cannot compute that boundary, so the learning rate is chosen by trying a few and watching the loss.

206
00:17:12.175 --> 00:17:20.225
If your loss goes to infinity or becomes "not a number" in the first few steps, the learning rate is almost always the reason.

207
00:17:20.825 --> 00:17:30.875
Here is a sanity check on the whole idea of learning, and I like it because it is the only case in this course where we can check gradient descent against an exact answer.

208
00:17:30.875 --> 00:17:36.325
Ordinary linear regression, fitting a straight line through points, has a direct solution.

209
00:17:36.325 --> 00:17:38.325
There is a formula.

210
00:17:38.325 --> 00:17:44.700
You put the numbers in and the best line comes out in one step, with no iteration and no learning rate.

211
00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:56.450
So take five noisy points lying roughly along y equals two x: x equals one to five, and y equals two point one, three point nine, six point two, seven point eight, ten point one.

212
00:17:56.450 --> 00:18:02.825
The exact solution gives an intercept of nought point zero five and a slope of one point nine nine.

213
00:18:02.825 --> 00:18:14.775
Then run gradient descent on the same data, with a learning rate of nought point zero one and a few thousand small steps, and it arrives at nought point zero five zero and one point nine nine zero.

214
00:18:14.775 --> 00:18:16.650
The same answer.

215
00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:23.150
That is reassuring, but it raises an obvious question: if there is a formula, why would anyone iterate?

216
00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:24.925
Two answers.

217
00:18:24.925 --> 00:18:29.100
The first is that almost no interesting model has a formula.

218
00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.675
A perceptron with a classification loss does not.

219
00:18:32.675 --> 00:18:36.150
A network with a hidden layer certainly does not.

220
00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:46.950
The exact solution exists for linear regression and for very few other things, and from this point in the course onwards, everything is learned iteratively because there is no alternative.

221
00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:51.725
The second answer is the one from the earlier slide, in a different costume.

222
00:18:51.725 --> 00:18:56.950
Deriving the answer requires you to know the structure of the problem well enough to solve it.

223
00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:00.825
Learning requires only that you can measure how wrong you are.

224
00:19:00.825 --> 00:19:06.075
That is a much weaker requirement, which is why it works on so many more problems.

225
00:19:06.675 --> 00:19:09.550
One loose end from the worked example.

226
00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:12.225
We used a single data point.

227
00:19:12.225 --> 00:19:14.550
Real training does not.

228
00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:22.000
In practice you have hundreds or millions of labelled examples, and the loss is the average of the per-example losses.

229
00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:28.350
The gradient is then the average of the per-example gradients, and the update rule is exactly the same.

230
00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:32.675
That leaves a choice: how many examples go into one update?

231
00:19:32.675 --> 00:19:35.525
You can use all of them for every step.

232
00:19:35.525 --> 00:19:40.625
The gradient is then as accurate as it can be, and the path downhill is smooth.

233
00:19:40.625 --> 00:19:47.775
But every single step costs a pass over the whole dataset, which for a large dataset is slow enough to be useless.

234
00:19:47.775 --> 00:19:51.900
Or you can use a small random handful, a batch, for each step.

235
00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:56.025
The gradient from a handful is noisy, so the path wanders.

236
00:19:56.025 --> 00:20:03.975
But you take hundreds of steps in the time the other method takes one, and the wandering turns out not to matter much, and sometimes helps.

237
00:20:03.975 --> 00:20:09.350
In practice everyone uses the second, with batches of a few dozen examples.

238
00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:14.350
The names you will hear are stochastic gradient descent, momentum, and Adam.

239
00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:20.250
I am naming them here only so that they are not new words when Session nine explains them properly.

240
00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:24.300
The mechanics you saw on the previous slides do not change at all.

241
00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:27.825
Only how many examples go into each gradient.

242
00:20:28.425 --> 00:20:30.725
Last piece of Block one.

243
00:20:30.725 --> 00:20:34.300
We have done the weighted sum, and we have done the learning.

244
00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:41.550
What sits between the sum and the answer is the activation function, and the choice of it decides what the model is called.

245
00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:43.425
The step function.

246
00:20:43.425 --> 00:20:47.150
Output one if z is positive, zero otherwise.

247
00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:50.550
The output is a class, a hard yes or no.

248
00:20:50.550 --> 00:21:00.375
A weighted sum with a step on the end is the original perceptron, and it is what we will use in Block two, because it makes the logic exact and easy to check by hand.

249
00:21:00.375 --> 00:21:06.400
Its fatal flaw, which I flagged earlier, is that its slope is zero everywhere it is defined.

250
00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:09.400
Gradient descent cannot train through it.

251
00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:10.950
The sigmoid.

252
00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:18.675
An S-shaped curve that squashes any number into the range zero to one, rising smoothly through a half at z equals zero.

253
00:21:18.675 --> 00:21:29.050
Because the output is between zero and one, you can read it as a probability rather than a verdict: not "this is class one" but "I am eighty-three per cent confident".

254
00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:38.700
A weighted sum with a sigmoid on the end is logistic regression, which is a method in its own right and which will keep turning up as a baseline for the rest of the course.

255
00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:41.525
And the sigmoid is a smoothed step.

256
00:21:41.525 --> 00:21:44.400
That is the whole reason it exists.

257
00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:50.650
It does what the step does, while having a slope everywhere, so gradient descent has something to work with.

258
00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:55.475
Then two more, which we will meet properly inside hidden layers in Block two.

259
00:21:55.475 --> 00:22:01.725
The hyperbolic tangent, tanh, which is a sigmoid rescaled to run from minus one to plus one.

260
00:22:01.725 --> 00:22:08.350
And the ReLU, which is simply the rule: if the number is negative, make it zero; otherwise leave it alone.

261
00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:13.950
That sounds too crude to be useful and it is the most used activation in deep learning.

262
00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:22.875
The thing to carry forward is this: the weighted sum is linear and always gives a flat boundary; the activation is where any bending can come from.

263
00:22:23.475 --> 00:22:28.125
A practical word before the break, so that the lab does not surprise you.

264
00:22:28.125 --> 00:22:32.425
In this session's lab you will implement gradient descent yourself.

265
00:22:32.425 --> 00:22:41.075
You will compute the gradient using automatic differentiation, which you met in Session two, and you will write the weight update as a line of code.

266
00:22:41.075 --> 00:22:46.075
There is no model object, no training-loop helper, no convenient one-liner.

267
00:22:46.075 --> 00:22:57.625
That is deliberate, and I want to say so out loud, because when you open the notebook you will notice that the code is longer than it needs to be and you may think you have been given the hard version by accident.

268
00:22:57.625 --> 00:23:02.550
The reason is that today is the only time you will see the machinery with the lid off.

269
00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:11.325
From Session eight you will use the library's model class and its optimiser, and they will do every one of those steps for you, correctly and invisibly.

270
00:23:11.325 --> 00:23:19.675
If you have never once watched a gradient being computed and applied, those libraries are magic, and when they go wrong you have nothing to think with.

271
00:23:19.675 --> 00:23:23.225
So: today you write it; from next week you call it.

